# Plan: Parse Credit Approval Data to CSV\
\
## Context\
The UCI Credit Approval dataset (`credit_approval/crx.data`) is already comma-delimited but has no header row. Column names and types are documented in `crx.names`. The goal is to produce a well-formed CSV with meaningful column names and correct handling of missing values (represented as `?` in the raw data).\
\
## Approach\
\
Create a Python script `credit_approval/parse_to_csv.py` that:\
\
1. Reads `crx.data` (690 rows, 16 columns, no header, delimiter `,`)\
2. Assigns column names based on `crx.names`:\
   - `A1` through `A15` (attributes), `A16` (class label)\
3. Replaces `?` (the UCI missing-value sentinel) with empty strings (standard CSV null)\
4. Writes the result to `credit_approval/credit_approval.csv`\
\
## Column Names\
\
| Column | Name | Type |\
|--------|------|------|\
| 1 | A1 | categorical (b, a) |\
| 2 | A2 | continuous |\
| 3 | A3 | continuous |\
| 4 | A4 | categorical (u, y, l, t) |\
| 5 | A5 | categorical (g, p, gg) |\
| 6 | A6 | categorical (c, d, cc, i, j, k, m, r, q, w, x, e, aa, ff) |\
| 7 | A7 | categorical (v, h, bb, j, n, z, dd, ff, o) |\
| 8 | A8 | continuous |\
| 9 | A9 | categorical (t, f) |\
| 10 | A10 | categorical (t, f) |\
| 11 | A11 | continuous |\
| 12 | A12 | categorical (t, f) |\
| 13 | A13 | categorical (g, p, s) |\
| 14 | A14 | continuous |\
| 15 | A15 | continuous |\
| 16 | A16 | class label (+, -) |\
\
## Critical Files\
\
- **Input**: `credit_approval/crx.data`\
- **Reference**: `credit_approval/crx.names`\
- **Output**: `credit_approval/credit_approval.csv`\
- **Script**: `credit_approval/parse_to_csv.py`\
\
## Script (pandas-based)\
\
```python\
import pandas as pd\
\
COLUMNS = ["A1","A2","A3","A4","A5","A6","A7","A8",\
           "A9","A10","A11","A12","A13","A14","A15","A16"]\
\
df = pd.read_csv("credit_approval/crx.data", header=None, names=COLUMNS, na_values="?")\
df.to_csv("credit_approval/credit_approval.csv", index=False)\
print(f"Saved \{len(df)\} rows to credit_approval/credit_approval.csv")\
```\
\
