\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 ICE plots (Individual Conditional Expectation)
\f0\b0 : PDP averages the effect over all individuals \'97 ICE shows each person's curve separately. If ICE lines fan out or cross, the feature's effect is heterogeneous, which means there's a meaningful interaction with something else. This is often more informative than PDP alone.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Conditional distributions by outcome
\f0\b0 : For each top feature, plot its distribution for approved vs. rejected cases (KDE for continuous, bar chart for categorical). This is model-free and serves as a sanity check \'97 the model's importance ranking should roughly align with how well each feature separates the two groups.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Direction/sign stability across folds
\f0\b0 : You're already tracking importance stability; also track the 
\f3\i direction
\f0\i0  of SHAP effects per fold. A feature that is consistently important but whose sign flips across folds is unreliable for interpretation.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Feature correlations among top features
\f0\b0 : Before interpreting individual importance scores, check pairwise correlations among the top features. Correlated features split importance between them \'97 if A2 and A3 are correlated and both appear in the top 5, their joint contribution is what matters, not their individual ranks. This also informs which pairs are worth examining with 2-way PDP.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 SHAP waterfall plots on borderline cases
\f0\b0 : Pick a handful of observations where the model is uncertain (predicted P \uc0\u8776  0.45\'960.55). A SHAP waterfall plot shows exactly which features pushed the decision in each direction for that specific case. This is very useful for building intuition about the model's logic at the margin, which is where credit decisions are actually consequential.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
