\f1\b \cf2 Where label encoding is fine:
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 RF performance
\f0\b0 : RF splits on thresholds, so for a nominal feature with 14 categories label-encoded as 0\'9613, it can only split into "\uc0\u8804 k vs >k" groups \'97 missing some valid partitions. But with 500 trees and random feature selection, it usually recovers enough. Feature importance (permutation/SHAP magnitude) is not affected by encoding at all.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 Where label encoding is genuinely problematic:
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Directional plots (SHAP dependence, beeswarm color)
\f0\b0 : the x-axis/color is the numeric code, not the category. "A6 = 7 \uc0\u8594  higher approval" is meaningless when 7 is an arbitrary label.\
\ls22\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Correlation heatmap
\f0\b0 : correlation between arbitrarily-ordered integers is noise for nominal features.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0

